{#- Likeness rules shared by character_sheet.v7 and character_portrait.v3 (production, 2026-10-19). v3 after the owner's
    live test (a night photo under yellow street light: a sepia watercolor, and a generic cartoon boy): the
    photo's light is not the child's color (COLOR), and the likeness notes, written from the photo by the text
    model before drawing (likeness_notes.v1), spell out this child's own hair, face, eyes and smile. v2: the exact
    age, neither younger nor older. -#}
{%- set he = 'He' if gender == 'm' else 'She' -%}
{%- set his = 'his' if gender == 'm' else 'her' -%}
LIKENESS (top priority: the parent must recognize their own child at once)
- Face: keep the photo's face shape and length, jaw and chin, cheeks, the shape, color and spacing of the eyes, the eyebrows, nose, mouth and smile, the ears, and distinctive features such as freckles, dimples, moles or missing teeth. Paint {{ his }} own face proportions, not a generic cute face; the eyes no bigger than in the photo, at most a little larger where the style asks for it.
{%- if not hijab %}
- Hair: exactly as in the photo: the same color, with its lighter and darker parts, the same length, direction and parting, the same texture (straight, wavy or curly) and the same shape at the front (fringe, quiff, or hair swept up, back or to the side). Never curlier, longer, darker or tidier than in the photo.
{%- endif %}
- Skin tone: exactly the child's own, as it looks in daylight, neither lighter nor darker.
- Age: {{ he }} is {{ age }} years old. Paint exactly {{ his }} age, neither younger nor older: a {{ age }}-year-old {{ 'boy' if gender == 'm' else 'girl' }} with {{ his }} own real proportions, not a toddler and not an older child.
{%- if glasses %}
- Glasses: {{ he | lower }} wears glasses; keep the same frame shape and color.
{%- endif %}
{%- if notes %}

WHAT THIS CHILD LOOKS LIKE (notes from the photo: keep every one in the drawing, in the style's own way)
{%- for line in notes %}
- {{ line }}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}

COLOR
The photo may be lit by a colored light (yellow or orange street light, a blue screen, a green wall) and may be dark or grainy. Ignore the photo's light, its color cast and its background: paint the child in clear, natural daylight colors, with {{ his }} true skin tone, hair color and eye color. The picture is clean and bright, never tinted brown, amber, yellow, sepia or gray.
